\subsection{Stress Sovereignty Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Stress Sovereignty]
Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the unified system and $\mathcal{I}$ the invariant
set. If all invariants remain locked under simultaneous maximum-load
perturbation across all domains, then the system achieves stress sovereignty,
defined by:
\[
\text{Integrity} = 100\%,\qquad
\text{Convergence} = \text{Absolute}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{D}$ denote the domain set:
\[
\mathcal{D} =
\{\text{Astrophysical},\ 
\text{Quantum-Granular},\ 
\text{Proteostatic},\ 
\text{Neural-Spatiotemporal},\ 
\text{Atmospheric}\}.
\]

Unified saturation requires:
\[
\forall d \in \mathcal{D},\quad
\text{Stress}(d) = \text{Maximum}.
\]

Invariant preservation requires:
\[
\forall I \in \mathcal{I},\quad
\frac{dI}{dt} \to 0.
\]

Since each invariant is coupled across domains, stability in one domain
propagates to all others:
\[
I_{\mathrm{Axon-FP}} =
I_{\mathrm{Janus-GRQFT}} =
I_{\mathrm{Mnemosyne}} =
I_{\mathrm{Chronos-V}} =
I_{\mathrm{Stratos-Core}}.
\]

Thus, no invariant exhibits drift or collapse, and the system maintains
absolute convergence. Stress sovereignty follows directly.
\end{proof}

